{
  "action": "question",
  "question_count": 1,
  "is_atomic": true,
  "atomic_questions": [
    "When a worker finishes their 2-week training, do they become fully productive (able to produce food I or II at the skilled-worker rate) starting the very next week, or is there any additional ramp-up period before they count as skilled?"
  ],
  "rationale": "The response contains a single clarification question asking for a specific policy choice regarding the timing of productivity after training."
}

{
  "action": "question",
  "question_count": 1,
  "is_atomic": true,
  "atomic_questions": [
    "Can a skilled worker who is currently training new workers also produce food in that same two-week period, or does training consume their full capacity so they produce nothing while training?"
  ],
  "rationale": "The response asks a single clarification question regarding whether training and production can occur simultaneously or if training is exclusive."
}

{
  "action": "question",
  "question_count": 1,
  "is_atomic": true,
  "atomic_questions": [
    "When a skilled worker trains 3 new workers over a two-week period, does that worker become available to train another group of up to 3 new workers in the immediately following two-week period, or is there a minimum gap between training assignments?"
  ],
  "rationale": "The response asks a single clarification question regarding the availability of a worker for consecutive training assignments."
}

{
  "action": "question",
  "question_count": 1,
  "is_atomic": true,
  "atomic_questions": [
    "does this mean 50 workers must have *completed* their 2-week training by the end of week 8 (so the last group must start by week 7), or is it enough that 50 workers have *started* training by the end of week 8 (allowing the last group to still be in training at week 8's end)?"
  ],
  "rationale": "The response asks for a single policy clarification regarding the definition of the training deadline (completion vs. start), which constitutes one independent question."
}

{
  "action": "question",
  "question_count": 1,
  "is_atomic": true,
  "atomic_questions": [
    "is the 540 yuan the *total* weekly wage for a worker who works 60 hours (i.e., it replaces the 360 yuan normal wage), or is it an *additional* overtime premium paid on top of the normal 360 yuan wage?"
  ],
  "rationale": "The response asks for a single policy clarification regarding the nature of the 540 yuan figure (total vs. additional), which constitutes one independent question."
}

{
  "action": "question",
  "question_count": 1,
  "is_atomic": true,
  "atomic_questions": [
    "Can a worker work overtime (60 hours) in every week of the 8-week horizon, or is there a limit on how many weeks a worker can work overtime?"
  ],
  "rationale": "The response asks a single clarification question regarding the policy on overtime frequency, presenting a binary choice between unlimited weekly overtime and a limited number of weeks."
}

{
  "action": "question",
  "question_count": 1,
  "is_atomic": true,
  "atomic_questions": [
    "does this penalty apply to any shortfall in a given week (i.e., if you produce less than that week's demand, you pay the rate times the unmet kg), or does it apply only to demand that is carried over to a later week (i.e., you can delay delivery and pay the penalty for each week of delay)?"
  ],
  "rationale": "The response asks for a single policy choice regarding the application of the late-delivery penalty, presenting two mutually exclusive interpretations as one question."
}

{
  "action": "question",
  "question_count": 1,
  "is_atomic": true,
  "atomic_questions": [
    "is it allowed to produce more than a week's demand and carry the surplus as inventory into the next week, or must production in each week exactly match (or fall short of) that week's demand with no storage?"
  ],
  "rationale": "The response asks for a single policy decision regarding inventory constraints, presenting two mutually exclusive options for one underlying question."
}

{
  "action": "question",
  "question_count": 1,
